# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# REDIRECTS — URL continuity through the revamp.
#
# Source: [W1_Redirect_Map.md], executed. Netlify/Cloudflare Pages format,
# matching public/_headers. Copied verbatim into dist/ at build.
#
# ⚠️ THESE RULES 404 ON LOCALHOST, AND THAT IS NOT A BUG. This is a HOST
#   file: Netlify and Cloudflare Pages parse it at deploy. `astro dev` and
#   `astro preview` do not — they serve static files and 404 on any path
#   without one, so every rule below looks broken locally.
#
#   ⭐ To exercise them on a laptop:
#       pnpm verify:redirects    # follows all 20: 301 → 200, no chains
#       pnpm preview:redirects   # serves dist/ WITH these applied, :4322
#
#   ⛔ Neither is a proof. Only `curl -I` against the deployed origin is
#   — see [W1 §4].
#
# ⚠️ THE LIVE SITE HAS 23 URLs IN A SUBMITTED SITEMAP. Three survive with
#   their path unchanged (/, /about/, /contact/) and need no rule, so
#   there are 20 rules here. ⛔ W1 §2: every one of the 23 gets a 301 or
#   keeps its path. NONE is left to 404, and NONE is bulk-redirected to /.
#   A blanket redirect to root is read by Google as a soft 404 and is
#   worse than a clean 410.
#
# ⛔ 301, NOT 302. These renames are permanent — a 302 tells search
#   engines to keep the old URL, which is the opposite of the intent.
#
# ⛔ NO CHAINS. Every source points at its FINAL destination. If a target
#   is ever renamed, re-point every rule that aimed at it rather than
#   adding a second hop.
#
# ⚠️ TRAILING SLASHES ON BOTH SIDES. astro.config.mjs sets
#   `trailingSlash: "always"`; a target without one costs a second
#   redirect and turns 20 rules into 40 hops.
#
# ⭐ RE-POINTING A 301 LATER IS FREE. Several rules below are many-to-one
#   into an index because the specific successor does not exist yet. When
#   one does, change the target here — that is cheaper than a 404 now.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════


